1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:01,980
Hello, I'm Tu Weikang.

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:05,280
This is my individual Final Project, TicketRoute.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,980
It helps bank support staff sort English customer messages

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,580
into 77 categories, with an option for human review.

5
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:18,080
It suggests a category without answering customers or accessing their
accounts.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,250
The problem is that manual sorting takes time.

7
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,140
If each message takes 15 seconds,

8
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:26,680
a thousand messages would take about four hours.

9
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:32,160
This is an assumption, not a measured saving.

10
00:00:32,620 --> 00:00:35,150
I built the interface and evaluation code in Python

11
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:37,720
and used GPT-5 mini through OpenRouter.

12
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:41,120
A single model call handles classification,

13
00:00:41,606 --> 00:00:45,666
while rules check the output and flag cases for review.

14
00:00:45,926 --> 00:00:48,486
I also tested a simple keyword baseline.

15
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:51,139
Let me show you three examples:

16
00:00:51,159 --> 00:00:53,019
a correct result,

17
00:00:53,139 --> 00:00:56,479
a case flagged for review,

18
00:00:56,859 --> 00:00:58,339
and a confident mistake.

19
00:00:58,586 --> 00:01:02,926
These replay saved responses from the earlier pilot,

20
00:01:02,966 --> 00:01:07,026
using its original 70% threshold.

21
00:01:07,472 --> 00:01:09,582
No new model calls happen here.

22
00:01:10,048 --> 00:01:15,048
After the pilot, I completed the full evaluation

23
00:01:15,108 --> 00:01:18,108
using the public BANKING77 dataset.

24
00:01:18,508 --> 00:01:22,848
This included about 2,000 validation queries,

25
00:01:22,908 --> 00:01:27,848
followed by 3,080 official test queries.

26
00:01:27,908 --> 00:01:32,258
Validation selected a 60% threshold,

27
00:01:32,668 --> 00:01:37,008
and I kept it unchanged for the official test.

28
00:01:37,657 --> 00:01:42,977
On the official test, the macro F1 score

29
00:01:42,997 --> 00:01:45,777
was 0.8489,

30
00:01:46,317 --> 00:01:49,757
above my target of 0.8.

31
00:01:50,314 --> 00:01:54,383
Accuracy reached 85.23%,

32
00:01:54,662 --> 00:01:58,582
compared with 38.08%

33
00:01:58,642 --> 00:02:01,102
for the keyword baseline.

34
00:02:01,778 --> 00:02:04,118
Still, the review rule has limits.

35
00:02:04,439 --> 00:02:07,455
It flagged 31 cases,

36
00:02:07,855 --> 00:02:11,625
including 19 incorrect predictions.

37
00:02:12,105 --> 00:02:17,935
But 436 mistakes

38
00:02:17,965 --> 00:02:21,125
still passed the threshold.

39
00:02:21,925 --> 00:02:26,605
So a high confidence score does not guarantee a correct answer.

40
00:02:27,578 --> 00:02:30,178
I also checked cost and response time.

41
00:02:30,698 --> 00:02:33,918
New API calls cost US$2.38,

42
00:02:34,438 --> 00:02:36,488
helped by prompt caching.

43
00:02:36,970 --> 00:02:39,690
The earlier pilot's cost was not recorded.

44
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:44,170
Median test latency was about 2.2 seconds.

45
00:02:44,564 --> 00:02:48,047
The program saves results, records charges,

46
00:02:48,087 --> 00:02:51,947
and stops on service errors or unknown billing.

47
00:02:52,355 --> 00:02:57,615
However, privacy controls and ongoing monitoring need further work.

48
00:02:57,935 --> 00:03:02,815
My next step would be a supervised trial before operational use.

49
00:03:03,239 --> 00:03:06,799
The code, data, and evaluation evidence

50
00:03:06,839 --> 00:03:09,119
are in my GitHub repository.

51
00:03:09,919 --> 00:03:13,619
AI tools helped with coding and drafting.

52
00:03:14,093 --> 00:03:18,553
I remain responsible for understanding and explaining the work.

53
00:03:18,793 --> 00:03:20,853
Thank you for watching.
